  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nA weak shortwave moving out of southeastern CO will bring increasing \ncloud cover and a few light radar returns to parts of southeast \nNebraska today. However, any rain associated with these is expected \nto remain aloft, as persistent dry low level air will likely \nevaporate the majority of precip. before it reaches the surface. \n\nAnother weak shortwave will ripple through the central Plains \nTuesday. While the vast majority of precipitation is expected to \nremain to our south, over Kansas, a few sprinkles or light showers \ncould survive the sub-cloud dry layer and reach the surface during \nthe evening and overnight hours. \n\nSoutherly low level flow will continue to advect warmer air into the \nregion this afternoon through Tuesday. Expect high temperatures to \nclimb into the upper 60s and low 70s today, with widespread low to \nmid 70s on Tuesday.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nUpper level ridging shifts eastward over the central CONUS Wednesday \nand Thursday, keeping highs about 10 degrees above normal, in the \nmid 70s. Dry weather will prevail through the rest of the work week.\n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nA strong trough will push through the intermountain west on Friday, \ntightening the pressure gradient, and causing southwesterly winds \nto surge across the central plains. Gusts up to 30-40+ mph can be \nexpected Friday afternoon through Saturday morning, before a cold \nfront brings winds around to the northwest. Temperatures on Friday \nwill remain in the mid 70s, but begin to fall behind the front on \nSaturday. Expect highs to range from the mid 60s in northeast \nNebraska to the low 70s in far southeast Nebraska/southwest Iowa, \nSaturday. \n\nShowers will move in behind the front, although models have been \nslowing down their arrival slightly over the last few runs. Expect \nrain chances to increase Saturday and diminish Sunday afternoon (30-\n50%). Temperatures remain closer to normal as we head into next \nweek, with highs forecast to top out in the mid 60s Sunday and \nMonday.",
